00:09:28.890 --> 00:09:31.110
my application was transferred
to a terminal master's

00:09:31.110 --> 00:09:31.943
program at the

00:09:31.943 --> 00:09:33.657
University of Chicago.
- Okay.

00:09:33.657 --> 00:09:35.670
- Where I was fortunate
to meet another person

00:09:35.670 --> 00:09:37.772
that was truly pivotal in my career.

00:09:37.772 --> 00:09:39.690
This is Dr. Harriet Dewitt,

00:09:39.690 --> 00:09:42.210
who's a human
psycho-pharmacology researcher.

00:09:42.210 --> 00:09:44.730
She kind of wanted to know how
drugs work in the brain and

00:09:44.730 --> 00:09:47.310
how they change behavior
and that kind of thing.

00:09:47.310 --> 00:09:51.212
And she really put me onto
the idea of neuroscience of

00:09:51.212 --> 00:09:52.830
addiction.

00:09:52.830 --> 00:09:55.620
And so after doing some studies
with her on alcohol and,

00:09:55.620 --> 00:09:57.420
and nicotine especially,

00:09:57.420 --> 00:09:59.790
and craving and things like
that and how that relates to

00:09:59.790 --> 00:10:01.170
relapse and such,

00:10:01.170 --> 00:10:05.400
I was fortunate to get into
the University of Michigan the

00:10:05.400 --> 00:10:07.980
second time I applied to
PhD programs and worked with

00:10:07.980 --> 00:10:09.060
Kent Burridge,

00:10:09.060 --> 00:10:13.320
who is a theorist of kind of
what does dopamine do in reward

00:10:13.320 --> 00:10:15.180
And behavior and so on.

00:10:15.180 --> 00:10:16.080
And so I did my,

00:10:16.080 --> 00:10:18.030
I didn't actually work on
dopamine too much there,

00:10:18.030 --> 00:10:19.710
but I did look at the, some of the,

00:10:19.710 --> 00:10:21.183
the brain substrates now in rats.

00:10:21.183 --> 00:10:23.370
- Okay
- Of addiction related

00:10:23.370 --> 00:10:25.510
behaviors or motivated behaviors then

00:10:25.510 --> 00:10:27.420
into a postdoc.

00:10:27.420 --> 00:10:30.060
I started to look more
specifically at addiction training

00:10:30.060 --> 00:10:32.910
