00:10:37.260 --> 00:10:40.050
why some individuals get
addicted when they try drugs and

00:10:40.050 --> 00:10:41.160
others don't.

00:10:41.160 --> 00:10:43.950
And that's kind of what's
been driving me since then in,

00:10:43.950 --> 00:10:46.500
since I've started my own
lab about eight years ago

00:10:46.500 --> 00:10:48.510
in Irvine, California.

00:10:48.510 --> 00:10:50.460
I've really been trying to dive
into those issues and what's

00:10:50.460 --> 00:10:52.920
the difference between, you
know, someone who gets addicted,

00:10:52.920 --> 00:10:56.233
someone who doesn't, someone
who has other kinds of,

00:10:56.233 --> 00:10:59.010
you know, problems in terms
of psychiatric disorders,

00:10:59.010 --> 00:11:00.900
depression, and so on as well.

00:11:00.900 --> 00:11:03.930
So that's kind of in a
nutshell where how I got here.

00:11:03.930 --> 00:11:04.890
- Yeah.

00:11:04.890 --> 00:11:05.723
That's great.

00:11:05.723 --> 00:11:08.400
We'll definitely appreciate the
work that you do in terms of

00:11:08.400 --> 00:11:09.240
understanding addiction,

00:11:09.240 --> 00:11:11.738
but then also some of the
coexistence with other psychiatric

00:11:11.738 --> 00:11:12.571
illnesses.

00:11:12.571 --> 00:11:15.510
But then also just appreciate
the context that you gave as

00:11:15.510 --> 00:11:16.343
well, I mean,

00:11:16.343 --> 00:11:18.150
I've said this several times
in several other contexts,

00:11:18.150 --> 00:11:20.520
but I think it's always helpful
to hear people's stories.

00:11:20.520 --> 00:11:21.528
I mean,

00:11:21.528 --> 00:11:22.440
there are aspects of your
story that I'm hearing for the

00:11:22.440 --> 00:11:24.084
first time.

00:11:24.084 --> 00:11:26.250
And also to learn about your
resilience and pivoting in the

00:11:26.250 --> 00:11:27.510
middle of that too, you know,

00:11:27.510 --> 00:11:30.030
applying to nine graduate
schools and not getting into any

00:11:30.030 --> 00:11:31.230
of them at the beginning.

00:11:31.230 --> 00:11:34.170
So I think sometimes there's
a tendency for all of us,

00:11:34.170 --> 00:11:37.800
but especially students or
trainees coming up to think that

00:11:37.800 --> 00:11:40.260
all the professors just kind
of had a straight path and that

00:11:40.260 --> 00:11:42.908
things just kind of all
fell in order and then,

00:11:42.908 --> 00:11:43.980
then you knew this is what
you were gonna do and then you

00:11:43.980 --> 00:11:46.770
stepped into this position
without knowing the journey along

00:11:46.770 --> 00:11:47.603
the way.

00:11:47.603 --> 00:11:49.830
So I imagine that people hearing
about that aspect of your

00:11:49.830 --> 00:11:52.560
journey and how you were able
to actually get a better sense

00:11:52.560 --> 00:11:54.720
of what you wanted will actually
help people who might be in

00:11:54.720 --> 00:11:57.450
the middle of feeling
confused or not knowing which

00:11:57.450 --> 00:11:59.550
direction to go or getting
rejected from a school and not

00:11:59.550 --> 00:12:00.500
knowing if they should continue.

00:12:00.500 --> 00:12:02.580
So I really appreciate that as well.

00:12:02.580 --> 00:12:04.420
- Yeah, I just note that
sometimes, you know,

00:12:04.420 --> 00:12:07.200
if I think if I had gotten into
grad school that first time,

00:12:07.200 --> 00:12:09.944
I don't think I would be
here sometimes, you know,

00:12:09.944 --> 00:12:12.330
life just happens the
way it does, you know,

00:12:12.330 --> 00:12:14.846
you could say for a reason or you know,

00:12:14.846 --> 00:12:16.830
it's been calling or
whatever you wanna call it.

00:12:16.830 --> 00:12:19.470
You know, sometimes your
best laid plans are,

00:12:19.470 --> 00:12:21.600
are not the way things go and
maybe that's for the best in

00:12:21.600 --> 00:12:22.470
the long run.

00:12:22.470 --> 00:12:24.081
- Yeah, really well said.

00:12:24.081 --> 00:12:25.710
Really well said.

00:12:25.710 --> 00:12:27.529
Deanna, how about you, your path,

00:12:27.529 --> 00:12:29.610
as we've heard a little
bit about from your bio,

00:12:29.610 --> 00:12:32.400
but it sounds like it wasn't
the path you predicted.

00:12:32.400 --> 00:12:34.447
- Definitely not, so
I went to high school,

00:12:34.447 --> 00:12:38.190
I grew up in rural Texas and
the town that I went to high

00:12:38.190 --> 00:12:39.023
school in,

00:12:39.023 --> 00:12:42.709
like no one really went to
college and most people didn't

00:12:42.709 --> 00:12:43.887
finish high school.

00:12:43.887 --> 00:12:46.807
And so I actually never did
graduate from high school.

00:12:46.807 --> 00:12:51.060
I thought I knew better,
I had a job in 12th grade,

00:12:51.060 --> 00:12:53.670
I was like, you know,
why go to school would,

00:12:53.670 --> 00:12:55.623
I can have like a car and you know,

00:12:56.820 --> 00:12:59.470
a job that pays minimum wage
seemed great at the time

00:13:01.055 --> 00:13:04.140
and didn't really see the
need to move forward, but oh,

00:13:04.140 --> 00:13:06.750
the year during which I should
have been finishing high

00:13:06.750 --> 00:13:09.600
school, I did decide to
go to college after all.

00:13:09.600 --> 00:13:11.430
And because I didn't have
a high school diploma,

00:13:11.430 --> 00:13:13.380
I wasn't really eligible
for most colleges.

00:13:13.380 --> 00:13:14.760
- Okay.
- So I ended up

00:13:14.760 --> 00:13:17.010
at St. John's College
in Santa Fe, New Mexico

00:13:17.010 --> 00:13:18.930
because they didn't require
a high school diploma or

00:13:18.930 --> 00:13:20.670
an SAT score.

00:13:20.670 --> 00:13:24.750
And I got there not really even
knowing what philosophy was.

00:13:24.750 --> 00:13:27.540
Everyone at St. John's gets
a degree in philosophy.

00:13:27.540 --> 00:13:29.970
I honestly thought I'd be
reading a lot of Kurt Vonnegut

00:13:29.970 --> 00:13:33.300
novels and instead I discovered
the philosophy was really

00:13:33.300 --> 00:13:35.820
quite difficult, but it
was a fantastic school,

00:13:35.820 --> 00:13:38.940
it was amazing, you know,
we read philosophy, we read,

00:13:38.940 --> 00:13:42.150
you know, scientific papers,
we read Newton's Principia

00:13:42.150 --> 00:13:44.550
we read all the quantum physics papers.

00:13:44.550 --> 00:13:46.230
They were incredible.

00:13:46.230 --> 00:13:47.670
And at the time I thought, oh,

00:13:47.670 --> 00:13:51.150
maybe I wanna be a physicist
not really knowing what all

00:13:51.150 --> 00:13:52.560
that entailed.

00:13:52.560 --> 00:13:54.780
And there was a professor at St. John's

00:13:54.780 --> 00:13:57.330
who used to take some
students up to Los Alamos

00:13:57.330 --> 00:14:01.770
National Laboratories
and at the laboratories,

00:14:01.770 --> 00:14:04.050
I met some physicists and they were okay,

00:14:04.050 --> 00:14:04.980
they weren't that friendly.

00:14:04.980 --> 00:14:07.380
But I met two physicians
who were working on viral

00:14:07.380 --> 00:14:09.867
disorders, viral diseases,
and I thought they were the,

00:14:09.867 --> 00:14:12.650
the greatest, so that's actually
where I decided to go to,

00:14:12.650 --> 00:14:16.290
to medical school
instead of anything else.

00:14:16.290 --> 00:14:17.670
And actually, similarly,

00:14:17.670 --> 00:14:19.560
I applied to all the
medical schools in Texas

00:14:19.560 --> 00:14:21.420
and got into none.
- Hmm.

00:14:21.420 --> 00:14:24.030
- But I got into medical
school in New York City at,

00:14:24.030 --> 00:14:27.450
at Cornell, and I remember
the day I interviewed,

00:14:27.450 --> 00:14:30.360
I wore a borrowed dress
and some high heels.

00:14:30.360 --> 00:14:32.393
And it was early January,

00:14:32.393 --> 00:14:35.550
it was probably one of
the worst days of my life.

00:14:35.550 --> 00:14:39.390
I'd only lived in Texas, Mexico,
and New Mexico before then.

00:14:39.390 --> 00:14:44.160
And I had never faced anything
like the gray sludgy slushy

00:14:44.160 --> 00:14:45.420
snow.

00:14:45.420 --> 00:14:48.120
And the interviews,

00:14:48.120 --> 00:14:50.430
each one was worse than the one before it.

00:14:50.430 --> 00:14:52.320
I thought for sure I wasn't in.

00:14:52.320 --> 00:14:55.500
And I met with the associate
dean who was in charge of

00:14:55.500 --> 00:14:57.810
admissions, who was an incredibly kind,

00:14:57.810 --> 00:15:01.080
thoughtful person who I do
think gave me the chance at,

00:15:01.080 --> 00:15:02.070
at this.

00:15:02.070 --> 00:15:05.310
And I told him that I was
never gonna come to this school

00:15:05.310 --> 00:15:07.473
and don't even bother to accept me.

00:15:07.473 --> 00:15:09.047
- Wow.
- And his response was really,

00:15:09.047 --> 00:15:11.872
you're gonna throw everything
away because of one bad day.

00:15:11.872 --> 00:15:14.400
Sure enough, I ended up at,

00:15:14.400 --> 00:15:17.070
at medical school and
even in medical school,

00:15:17.070 --> 00:15:20.340
my plan was to return to Texas
and be a primary care doctor

00:15:20.340 --> 00:15:21.930
for rural Texas.

00:15:21.930 --> 00:15:24.561
By the end of the four
years I was like, no,

00:15:24.561 --> 00:15:27.120
I'm gonna do psychiatry instead.

00:15:27.120 --> 00:15:29.970
And then I ended up in
neuroscience research and it was

00:15:29.970 --> 00:15:32.100
definitely, you know, some
accidents along the way,

00:15:32.100 --> 00:15:35.100
a lot of, you know, as
Steve had mentioned, some,

00:15:35.100 --> 00:15:38.433
you know, some, you know,
powerful mentors along the way.

00:15:39.480 --> 00:15:42.123
And that's how I ended up here.

00:15:42.123 --> 00:15:43.290
- Wow, that's great to hear.

00:15:43.290 --> 00:15:44.787
I mean, definitely appreciate
that context as well.

00:15:44.787 --> 00:15:47.010
And so many parallels in your stories too,

00:15:47.010 --> 00:15:49.260
which I did not anticipate, just in terms,

00:15:49.260 --> 00:15:51.000
definitely in terms of
not what you had planned,

00:15:51.000 --> 00:15:52.500
but even in terms of, you know,

00:15:52.500 --> 00:15:55.050
not getting into schools you'd
apply to the strong role as

00:15:55.050 --> 00:15:57.190
you mentioned of mentors
really kind of shaping and

00:15:57.190 --> 00:16:01.020
redirecting you and just
having, again, some of that,

00:16:01.020 --> 00:16:03.270
that resilience too,
so I think that speaks,

00:16:03.270 --> 00:16:05.610
that speaks volumes, as a follow up,

00:16:05.610 --> 00:16:10.350
I'm curious how you think your
path actually impacts you on

00:16:10.350 --> 00:16:12.270
a day-to-day basis.

00:16:12.270 --> 00:16:13.103
Just like the,

00:16:13.103 --> 00:16:15.316
the navigation that you had
to take to get to the current

00:16:15.316 --> 00:16:19.380
stage and some of the
serendipity, if you wanna call it.

00:16:19.380 --> 00:16:21.210
Like how does that actually
impact what you do in your

00:16:21.210 --> 00:16:24.240
perspective, and Diana,
we can start with you.

00:16:24.240 --> 00:16:26.735
- You know, I definitely
view it as, you know,

00:16:26.735 --> 00:16:29.490
I think a lot of life is hard work,

00:16:29.490 --> 00:16:32.850
but a lot of it is luck and
just things sort of lining up.

00:16:32.850 --> 00:16:35.760
And I'm always incredibly
grateful for that.

00:16:35.760 --> 00:16:38.360
And you know, the joke in
my family is that, you know,

00:16:38.360 --> 00:16:40.470
if there had been one missed opportunity,

00:16:40.470 --> 00:16:43.770
I'd probably be working at the
Walmart in Falfurrias Texas,

00:16:43.770 --> 00:16:46.440
although my mom says I'd be the manager.

00:16:46.440 --> 00:16:49.590
So, and that.

00:16:49.590 --> 00:16:52.702
- Seems appropriate for mom
to say and probably true.

00:16:52.702 --> 00:16:53.937
- You know, it's just,

00:16:53.937 --> 00:16:56.403
and so I try to be mindful
of that like, and we,

00:16:57.630 --> 00:16:59.040
we all tend to,

00:16:59.040 --> 00:17:00.810
things we've earned feel very earned.

00:17:00.810 --> 00:17:05.730
But really like so much is just
chance and I'm grateful for

00:17:05.730 --> 00:17:08.310
those chances and recognize
that that doesn't happen all the

00:17:08.310 --> 00:17:11.340
time to other people and to, you know,

00:17:11.340 --> 00:17:14.687
with small circumstances for
us had incredibly powerfully

00:17:14.687 --> 00:17:16.380
positive outcomes.

00:17:16.380 --> 00:17:18.717
But sometimes it goes through
the way and that's why I see a

00:17:18.717 --> 00:17:20.550
lot in my research studies people have

00:17:20.550 --> 00:17:21.873
substance use disorders.

00:17:23.160 --> 00:17:24.900
- Yeah, that's really helpful perspective.

00:17:24.900 --> 00:17:26.790
Steve, what about you?

00:17:26.790 --> 00:17:27.623
- Well,

00:17:27.623 --> 00:17:30.600
I certainly resonate with the
idea of both hard work and

00:17:30.600 --> 00:17:33.840
luck being extremely important
in this kind of career.

00:17:33.840 --> 00:17:35.450
This is something I
always try to convey to,

00:17:35.450 --> 00:17:38.640
to trainees is that there's
some things you can control and

00:17:38.640 --> 00:17:42.119
some things you can't and you
kind of wanna try to learn

00:17:42.119 --> 00:17:44.460
what is the difference between
those things and then put

00:17:44.460 --> 00:17:47.460
yourself in positions
where you can make choices.

00:17:47.460 --> 00:17:49.380
This is, to me, the best
career advice that I,

00:17:49.380 --> 00:17:51.150
I know how to give, I think is,

00:17:51.150 --> 00:17:53.760
is putting yourself in a place
where you have not just one

00:17:53.760 --> 00:17:54.870
option that you must do.

00:17:54.870 --> 00:17:56.820
Because that's the only thing on a table.

00:17:56.820 --> 00:17:58.710
If you can get yourself in
a place where you can choose

00:17:58.710 --> 00:18:01.590
between A and B, that's
almost always gonna be better.

00:18:01.590 --> 00:18:05.490
And also being open to things
that you didn't expect to be

00:18:05.490 --> 00:18:06.750
the choice you're about to make.

00:18:06.750 --> 00:18:07.871
You know, those are,

00:18:07.871 --> 00:18:09.210
those are, that's certainly
how my career came about.

00:18:09.210 --> 00:18:12.467
I've seen many other
scientists that have that,

00:18:12.467 --> 00:18:14.640
that aspect to their careers as well.

00:18:14.640 --> 00:18:15.990
They just, you know,
being open-minded and,

00:18:15.990 --> 00:18:17.820
and taking opportunities when they,

00:18:17.820 --> 00:18:20.490
when they come or when you
put yourself in front of 'em.

00:18:20.490 --> 00:18:23.790
And then I guess in terms
of how my, you know,

00:18:23.790 --> 00:18:26.850
history affects my current
perspective on science,

00:18:26.850 --> 00:18:30.690
I certainly consider myself a
psychologist even though I do

00:18:30.690 --> 00:18:33.750
studies in rats and a lot
of it is very, you know,

00:18:33.750 --> 00:18:36.660
molecular neuroscience,
genetics and so on.

00:18:36.660 --> 00:18:38.940
I'm very much coming from a
psychological perspective and

00:18:38.940 --> 00:18:41.940
that's really how I try to approach the,

00:18:41.940 --> 00:18:45.000
the rat brain trying to
understand what are psychological

00:18:45.000 --> 00:18:47.670
processes that might exist in
both humans and rats and how

00:18:47.670 --> 00:18:49.680
can we, you know, if we can
understand them then in rats,

00:18:49.680 --> 00:18:51.600
maybe we can get some
insights into humans.

00:18:51.600 --> 00:18:53.280
But also not expecting
that that's always going

00:18:53.280 --> 00:18:54.113
to be the case.
- Mm.

00:18:54.113 --> 00:18:55.950
- I think it's really
important to recognize

00:18:55.950 --> 00:18:57.960
they're not just little furry humans.

00:18:57.960 --> 00:18:59.700
And even when they, for example,

00:18:59.700 --> 00:19:02.190
take drugs voluntarily in my lab,

00:19:02.190 --> 00:19:04.290
which is what humans
are doing in addiction,

00:19:04.290 --> 00:19:06.510
there are some key differences
in most of the studies that

00:19:06.510 --> 00:19:07.432
people do.

00:19:07.432 --> 00:19:08.970
For example,

00:19:08.970 --> 00:19:10.860
the rats don't have much
else going on in their lives,

00:19:10.860 --> 00:19:12.030
so why wouldn't they take drugs?

00:19:12.030 --> 00:19:14.970
Maybe it's sort of not a
disorder for them in some ways.

00:19:14.970 --> 00:19:15.960
So these are the kinds of questions I,

00:19:15.960 --> 00:19:19.080
I really think about a lot in
my own lab and ways to try to

00:19:19.080 --> 00:19:22.140
improve our rat models and
that certainly comes from my,

00:19:22.140 --> 00:19:23.970
my training history and
the experiences I've had

00:19:23.970 --> 00:19:25.620
along the way.

00:19:25.620 --> 00:19:26.453
- Yeah.

00:19:26.453 --> 00:19:29.220
Really great perspective and
appreciate the words of wisdom

00:19:29.220 --> 00:19:31.828
that you all are sharing for
people coming up as well.

00:19:31.828 --> 00:19:34.170
'Cause I think again, it's a
theme that comes up repeatedly.

00:19:34.170 --> 00:19:37.080
I mean I definitely hear that
from more senior scientists.

00:19:37.080 --> 00:19:38.282
So even thinking about
some of the folks at Yale,

00:19:38.282 --> 00:19:40.958
Dr. George Heninger is one
that comes to mind who really

00:19:40.958 --> 00:19:43.230
facilitated and started a lot of molecular

00:19:43.230 --> 00:19:44.490
psychiatry work there.

00:19:44.490 --> 00:19:47.100
But hearing his story and how
there were so many things that

00:19:47.100 --> 00:19:49.320
he didn't anticipate and
basically him just saying,

00:19:49.320 --> 00:19:50.250
saying like both of you,

00:19:50.250 --> 00:19:52.830
that he was just ready for
the opportunities that came,

00:19:52.830 --> 00:19:55.770
even though things that he
really wanted to do fell apart,

00:19:55.770 --> 00:19:58.230
things that he never planned
to do opened up and just being

00:19:58.230 --> 00:20:00.510
ready to kind of jump
into those opportunities.

00:20:00.510 --> 00:20:02.190
So I appreciate the way you both mentioned

00:20:02.190 --> 00:20:03.720
that context as well.

00:20:03.720 --> 00:20:04.553
And again,

00:20:04.553 --> 00:20:07.230
it seems like it's impacting
your work in so many ways,

00:20:07.230 --> 00:20:09.510
even as you think about
how you frame things as a

00:20:09.510 --> 00:20:12.000
psychologist who's doing
neuroscience or as a physician.

00:20:12.000 --> 00:20:13.500
And so I think that's really,

00:20:13.500 --> 00:20:16.050
really healthy as well to pick up, Steve,

00:20:16.050 --> 00:20:18.180
where you left off in terms
of some of the addiction work

00:20:18.180 --> 00:20:21.030
and things like that, it's gonna
be a pretty hard pivot but,

00:20:21.030 --> 00:20:21.870
or a quick pivot.

00:20:21.870 --> 00:20:24.129
But I'd be curious if you both
also could talk a little bit

00:20:24.129 --> 00:20:29.129
about what you think the role
of science is for society.

00:20:29.580 --> 00:20:30.942
And Steve,

00:20:30.942 --> 00:20:32.730
you already hinted at that in
terms of some things translate

00:20:32.730 --> 00:20:34.920
to humans, some things don't, like what,

00:20:34.920 --> 00:20:36.450
what do you see is that role?

00:20:36.450 --> 00:20:38.310
And to layer it a little bit,

00:20:38.310 --> 00:20:39.930
and you can come back to this if you want,

00:20:39.930 --> 00:20:43.050
also just thinking about all
the mixed feelings that people

00:20:43.050 --> 00:20:47.188
have in society in terms of
pro-science, anti-science,

00:20:47.188 --> 00:20:49.830
rodents in science,

00:20:49.830 --> 00:20:52.200
and is that helpful or are we
doing things that we shouldn't

00:20:52.200 --> 00:20:53.033
be doing?

00:20:53.033 --> 00:20:54.930
So I know that's a whole nother
layer so you don't have to

00:20:54.930 --> 00:20:56.580
go too much into that, we can
come back to that as well,

00:20:56.580 --> 00:20:57.930
but I wanted to at least put it in that

00:20:57.930 --> 00:20:59.343
broader context as well.

00:21:00.360 --> 00:21:01.456
- Yeah.

00:21:01.456 --> 00:21:04.770
So I guess there's sort
of two things that,

00:21:04.770 --> 00:21:07.440
that are behind my drive to,

00:21:07.440 --> 00:21:09.510
to ask the kinds of
questions that I try to ask

