of coming from different
backgrounds and things and who then

00:42:04.470 --> 00:42:07.470
can bring in their own
perspectives and ideas,

00:42:07.470 --> 00:42:09.030
new ideas.

00:42:09.030 --> 00:42:11.250
This is another path
forward I think is to,

00:42:11.250 --> 00:42:14.580
is to really try something
a little different.

00:42:14.580 --> 00:42:16.041
You know,

00:42:16.041 --> 00:42:17.010
I would argue that our
neuroscience that we've had so far

00:42:17.010 --> 00:42:19.740
has not been very successful
at helping people or not as

00:42:19.740 --> 00:42:20.820
much as it could.

00:42:20.820 --> 00:42:22.860
And I think this might be part
of the problem is we are a

00:42:22.860 --> 00:42:26.280
little bit too heavily focused
on the exact thing that,

00:42:26.280 --> 00:42:28.800
you know, we've always
studied and I think we,

00:42:28.800 --> 00:42:29.793
we need new ideas.

00:42:30.951 --> 00:42:32.523
- Yeah, really well said.

00:42:33.427 --> 00:42:34.446
And Diana

00:42:34.446 --> 00:42:35.279
I don't know if I'm putting
you on the spot here,

00:42:35.279 --> 00:42:36.112
but as Steve mentioned,

00:42:36.112 --> 00:42:39.030
that makes me think of the
philosophy talk that you gave at

00:42:39.030 --> 00:42:43.170
an addiction conference to
neuroscientist and how much of a

00:42:43.170 --> 00:42:44.400
shift that was, and that's,

00:42:44.400 --> 00:42:46.350
I mean that's why Steve and I
had such a long conversation

00:42:46.350 --> 00:42:49.410
afterwards because forcing
us to think about things in a

00:42:49.410 --> 00:42:51.300
different way that we
don't always think about.

00:42:51.300 --> 00:42:53.430
Although I feel like some of it was there,

00:42:53.430 --> 00:42:55.980
but when most of us just don't
articulate it or take the

00:42:55.980 --> 00:42:57.450
time to actually delve into that.

00:42:57.450 --> 00:42:59.130
So I'm curious if you, you know,

00:42:59.130 --> 00:43:00.870
on the spot we wanted to share
at least some of the things

00:43:00.870 --> 00:43:02.730
that you've been thinking
about around those realms.

00:43:02.730 --> 00:43:04.950
So just this one example of
integrating from different

00:43:04.950 --> 00:43:05.783
perspectives.

00:43:06.930 --> 00:43:09.690
- Yeah, so when I was asked
to give that talk, because I,

00:43:09.690 --> 00:43:12.750
my undergraduate degree was
in philosophy and when I was

00:43:12.750 --> 00:43:14.760
asked to give the talk, my
first reaction was like, yeah,

00:43:14.760 --> 00:43:15.870
sure, why not?

00:43:15.870 --> 00:43:18.736
And then I thought about it,
I'm like, what am I gonna say?

00:43:18.736 --> 00:43:21.180
And honestly, like I spent probably,

00:43:21.180 --> 00:43:23.290
I spent many months working on that talk

00:43:24.175 --> 00:43:25.980
it was really like really you
thought long and hard about

00:43:25.980 --> 00:43:28.500
it, 'cause like I, like, I
dunno what I'm gonna say.

00:43:28.500 --> 00:43:32.160
And it actually had a real
change in my career thinking

00:43:32.160 --> 00:43:35.100
about that talk because what
I really tried to think about

00:43:35.100 --> 00:43:38.700
in that talk was what do we
mean when we think of bad?

00:43:38.700 --> 00:43:41.760
What do we mean when we think
of bad behavior and we think

00:43:41.760 --> 00:43:44.130
of, you know, injury to
self or injury to others.

00:43:44.130 --> 00:43:48.030
And so I try to take examples
of psychiatric disorders that

00:43:48.030 --> 00:43:50.160
involve harm to others.
- Hmm.

00:43:50.160 --> 00:43:51.840
- And the top of that list is, you know,

00:43:51.840 --> 00:43:54.570
antisocial personality
disorder and pedophilia.

00:43:54.570 --> 00:43:56.272
And I ask myself, you know,

00:43:56.272 --> 00:43:58.650
are these brain disorders?

00:43:58.650 --> 00:44:01.530
And did a lot of research on them,

00:44:01.530 --> 00:44:04.860
have started a new project
looking at antisocial personality

00:44:04.860 --> 00:44:09.090
disorder and view it as being
antisocial and a personality

00:44:09.090 --> 00:44:11.640
disorder, but really if you
break it down into its pieces,

00:44:11.640 --> 00:44:16.350
it comes down to impulse
control, impulsivity.

00:44:16.350 --> 00:44:18.750
And I think there's a lot
of data to suggest that it,

00:44:18.750 --> 00:44:23.727
that there's difficulty
understanding social situations.

00:44:23.727 --> 00:44:25.170
And most people have anti-social
personality disorder,

00:44:25.170 --> 00:44:28.560
they don't understand the
situation well and when it doesn't

00:44:28.560 --> 00:44:32.430
go in the way that they expect
it to, the reaction is anger.

00:44:32.430 --> 00:44:36.330
And we all have situations
in which we have social

00:44:36.330 --> 00:44:38.940
interaction that doesn't
go the way we expect it to.

00:44:38.940 --> 00:44:42.690
And sometimes we react
with sadness or shame or

00:44:42.690 --> 00:44:45.767
embarrassment or anger.

00:44:45.767 --> 00:44:46.800
And when we react with anger,

00:44:46.800 --> 00:44:49.080
it's because we think we're right.

00:44:49.080 --> 00:44:51.240
So when you and I have a social
interaction that doesn't go

00:44:51.240 --> 00:44:53.940
well and we react with
something other than anger,

00:44:53.940 --> 00:44:56.760
we think that, oh, maybe
we're wrong, right?

00:44:56.760 --> 00:44:59.910
So somebody who reacts with
anger tends to not understand

00:44:59.910 --> 00:45:01.590
what happened.

00:45:01.590 --> 00:45:04.080
And so I've been trying to do
more research on anti-social

00:45:04.080 --> 00:45:06.510
personality disorder
looking at impulsivity

00:45:06.510 --> 00:45:08.670
and social cognition.

00:45:08.670 --> 00:45:09.768
So I'm trying to start that up.

00:45:09.768 --> 00:45:10.670
And then I also,

00:45:10.670 --> 00:45:13.110
in that talk I talked about
pedophilia, which was,

00:45:13.110 --> 00:45:14.280
that was tough, you know,

00:45:14.280 --> 00:45:15.690
like I asked myself the question is

00:45:15.690 --> 00:45:16.950
pedophilia a brain disorder?

00:45:16.950 --> 00:45:19.620
And I think I mentioned like
my first reaction was like,

00:45:19.620 --> 00:45:21.720
Lord, I don't know and I don't wanna know.

00:45:22.600 --> 00:45:24.390
My second reaction was I really
don't want the FBI banging

00:45:24.390 --> 00:45:25.263
down my door.

00:45:26.160 --> 00:45:28.380
But you know, I've been looking up and,

00:45:28.380 --> 00:45:29.430
and reading more about it.

00:45:29.430 --> 00:45:32.820
It's deeply understudied
and honestly, you know, we,

00:45:32.820 --> 00:45:36.300
we shy away from it but it is
a brain disorder and honestly

00:45:36.300 --> 00:45:38.010
what would be the worst that happens

00:45:38.010 --> 00:45:39.390
if we were to find a treatment?

00:45:39.390 --> 00:45:42.990
There may be fewer future victims
and yet we still can't get

00:45:42.990 --> 00:45:44.850
our head around it, it's
completely understudied.

00:45:44.850 --> 00:45:45.683
It's underfunded.

00:45:45.683 --> 00:45:47.800
There hasn't been a
grant funded since 2008.

00:45:49.010 --> 00:45:50.504
What's interesting to me about pedophilia,

00:45:50.504 --> 00:45:51.965
and this is a study I
wanna do down the road,

00:45:51.965 --> 00:45:53.190
although hopefully no one's
listening for my institution

00:45:53.190 --> 00:45:55.470
'cause I think I'll have a
freak out if I do this study.

00:45:55.470 --> 00:45:58.830
But there is some hint to
suggest that there's a lot of

00:45:58.830 --> 00:46:02.550
overlap between obsessive
thinking and pedophilia.

00:46:02.550 --> 00:46:05.010
And I wonder if we could
look at it more from the

00:46:05.010 --> 00:46:08.940
perspective of OCD especially at least in,

00:46:08.940 --> 00:46:12.780
in patients who want treatment,
which is the place to start.

00:46:12.780 --> 00:46:15.930
So even though I mostly do
neuroscience and addiction and

00:46:15.930 --> 00:46:16.763
certainly, you know,

00:46:16.763 --> 00:46:19.170
antisocial personality disorder
has a lot of overlap with

00:46:19.170 --> 00:46:20.970
addiction and I think pedophilia does too.

00:46:20.970 --> 00:46:24.660
I think there's a lot of substance
use we don't really know.

00:46:24.660 --> 00:46:26.355
I'm starting to move and,

00:46:26.355 --> 00:46:28.620
and look and see if we can get
beyond just thinking of these

00:46:28.620 --> 00:46:32.370
as being fixed bad brains
into thinking like,

00:46:32.370 --> 00:46:35.130
can we break it down into the
symptoms because we know how

00:46:35.130 --> 00:46:36.753
to treat some of these symptoms.

00:46:37.950 --> 00:46:40.320
- Yeah, that's really, really
well said and I appreciate,

00:46:40.320 --> 00:46:42.210
I mean there's so many pieces
that you brought up too,

00:46:42.210 --> 00:46:44.550
just even in terms of, you
know, flying out for what,

00:46:44.550 --> 00:46:46.290
what Steven was mentioning
about the ways that we

00:46:46.290 --> 00:46:48.780
communicate, but then
even a layer beneath that,

00:46:48.780 --> 00:46:51.660
what do we actually think
about and investigate and study

00:46:51.660 --> 00:46:54.653
and what do we shy away from
and all the reasons, Steve,

00:46:54.653 --> 00:46:57.150
if you look like you might
have a comment to jump in with.

00:46:57.150 --> 00:47:00.660
- Yeah, I mean it's just
fascinating and obviously like,

00:47:00.660 --> 00:47:01.693
you know,

00:47:01.693 --> 00:47:04.110
pedophilia I think is not
something we do talk about very

00:47:04.110 --> 00:47:05.850
much as a scientific field.

00:47:05.850 --> 00:47:08.970
And I think the reason is because
the first thing that comes

00:47:08.970 --> 00:47:13.440
to mind is, you know,
disgust or you know these,

00:47:13.440 --> 00:47:16.579
maybe people are evil or
there's something like really,

00:47:16.579 --> 00:47:17.640
you know, bad about them and so on.

00:47:17.640 --> 00:47:20.640
And that's the case in a very real way.

00:47:20.640 --> 00:47:23.880
And I would say that that is
to some extent also the case

00:47:23.880 --> 00:47:25.680
with addiction, many times we,

00:47:25.680 --> 00:47:28.410
even though we know that there
is ultimately in some way

00:47:28.410 --> 00:47:30.450
addiction is a brain disorder as well,

00:47:30.450 --> 00:47:33.090
it does cause people to do
terrible things to other people

00:47:33.090 --> 00:47:34.650
and to themselves certainly.

00:47:34.650 --> 00:47:37.560
And I think that's also a little
bit an aspect of a lot of a

00:47:37.560 --> 00:47:38.430
psychiatric disorder.

00:47:38.430 --> 00:47:40.410
Something wrong with you
that's like a bad thing.

00:47:40.410 --> 00:47:41.760
There's something bad about you.

00:47:41.760 --> 00:47:44.389
And I don't think we really
have that impression of other

00:47:44.389 --> 00:47:47.100
types of brain disorders
like Alzheimer's disease for

00:47:47.100 --> 00:47:48.870
example, or Parkinson's disease.

00:47:48.870 --> 00:47:51.570
I don't think anyone really
is frequently blaming those

00:47:51.570 --> 00:47:52.590
people and thinking they're,

00:47:52.590 --> 00:47:55.560
they're bad because their brain
is broken and it's causing

00:47:55.560 --> 00:47:58.230
them to have, you know, lots of problems.

00:47:58.230 --> 00:48:00.540
And I'd argue that's because
we understand those disorders a

00:48:00.540 --> 00:48:01.373
little bit better.

00:48:01.373 --> 00:48:03.685
We have something that is
biological that it like actually

00:48:03.685 --> 00:48:06.630
causes it, whereas in,
for example, addiction,

00:48:06.630 --> 00:48:09.930
people are just making bad
choices and that is the disorder

00:48:09.930 --> 00:48:11.520
in some ways, and that's, you know,

00:48:11.520 --> 00:48:13.500
hard to get your head around
how this thing could be,

00:48:13.500 --> 00:48:15.240
you know, how a person can, you know,

00:48:15.240 --> 00:48:18.247
kind of have free will and yet
still be controlled by their,

00:48:18.247 --> 00:48:19.830
you know, brain in some way.

00:48:19.830 --> 00:48:21.000
And I don't know,

00:48:21.000 --> 00:48:23.100
it's just these are really
tough topics to wrestle with and

00:48:23.100 --> 00:48:24.944
obviously especially for,

00:48:24.944 --> 00:48:26.610
for something as sensitive as pedophilia.

00:48:26.610 --> 00:48:27.480
- Yeah.

00:48:27.480 --> 00:48:29.580
Yeah, I mean definitely like,

00:48:29.580 --> 00:48:31.650
even as I'm listening to both of you talk,

00:48:31.650 --> 00:48:34.470
this kind of ties everything
back together 'cause it speaks

00:48:34.470 --> 00:48:37.080
to both how much more we
need to be talking about

00:48:37.080 --> 00:48:39.526
and thinking about and
investigating these things.

00:48:39.526 --> 00:48:41.130
So if we're not even doing that,

00:48:41.130 --> 00:48:44.010
how can we actually communicate
it to the public if we can't

00:48:44.010 --> 00:48:46.500
even have those conversations ourselves?

00:48:46.500 --> 00:48:48.588
So, you know, Diana here,

00:48:48.588 --> 00:48:49.650
what you're already wrestling
through and trying to think

00:48:49.650 --> 00:48:52.350
about that type of work,
I think is so important.

00:48:52.350 --> 00:48:53.310
And it gets back to, I mean,

00:48:53.310 --> 00:48:56.370
the work that we have to do
internally in a sense and also

00:48:56.370 --> 00:48:59.010
externally, not that any
of us can do it on our own,

00:48:59.010 --> 00:49:02.130
but something that we kind of
have to do in collaboration

00:49:02.130 --> 00:49:04.170
with one another, so, I
mean, I think this is,

00:49:04.170 --> 00:49:06.570
it's helpful because it's a
good, it's a challenge point.

00:49:06.570 --> 00:49:08.400
It speaks to the hope
that can come from that,

00:49:08.400 --> 00:49:10.920
but also the reality of the
fact that there's still work to

00:49:10.920 --> 00:49:12.213
be done in a lot of ways.

00:49:14.697 --> 00:49:15.870
- And certainly, you know,

00:49:15.870 --> 00:49:18.630
when it comes to antisocial
personality disorder and which I

00:49:18.630 --> 00:49:19.830
mean, you know,

00:49:19.830 --> 00:49:23.040
most of my participants here
in my studies will most all

00:49:23.040 --> 00:49:26.430
have substance use disorders
and a fair number really do

00:49:26.430 --> 00:49:30.510
have either anti-social
personality disorder or in that

00:49:30.510 --> 00:49:31.950
sort of range of things.

00:49:31.950 --> 00:49:34.110
And I don't mean people
who completely lack,

00:49:34.110 --> 00:49:36.480
we often think of anti-social
personality disorders,

00:49:36.480 --> 00:49:38.160
completely lacking empathy.

00:49:38.160 --> 00:49:39.500
That's not actually really the case.

00:49:39.500 --> 00:49:42.120
It was a really nice study
done recently that interviewed

00:49:42.120 --> 00:49:45.300
3000 people with anti-social
personality disorder and only

00:49:45.300 --> 00:49:49.169
20% reported symptoms
like lack of empathy,

00:49:49.169 --> 00:49:52.230
cruelty to animals and bullying.

00:49:52.230 --> 00:49:54.840
Most of the symptoms are
things like, you know,

00:49:54.840 --> 00:49:56.670
not paying rent, not following through,

00:49:56.670 --> 00:50:00.450
quitting a job without a job
to follow up with, you know,

00:50:00.450 --> 00:50:04.047
stealing, you know,
certainly behaviors that are,

00:50:04.047 --> 00:50:08.010
are problematic but aren't
necessarily sort of like a,

00:50:08.010 --> 00:50:11.580
you know, a monster like
lack of empathy or sympathy.

00:50:11.580 --> 00:50:14.430
And so I do think we have an
obligation for this patient

00:50:14.430 --> 00:50:17.940
population because studies
show that 40% of patients with

00:50:17.940 --> 00:50:20.220
opioid use disorder have ASPD.

00:50:20.220 --> 00:50:22.560
And if we can treat this part of it,

00:50:22.560 --> 00:50:25.530
then 40% of people may not
respond to our treatments.

00:50:25.530 --> 00:50:27.630
- Yeah.
- To bring it back to rodents,

00:50:27.630 --> 00:50:30.557
I think one thing that we do
need to be looking at, and I,

00:50:30.557 --> 00:50:32.160
and something that I think is
happening in the rodent field,

00:50:32.160 --> 00:50:34.350
y'all have to tell me
more, is sort of, you know,

00:50:34.350 --> 00:50:35.390
looking at the role of,

00:50:35.390 --> 00:50:38.400
of social stress and social
status and social and how that

00:50:38.400 --> 00:50:41.490
fits, and when it comes to
substance use disorders,

00:50:41.490 --> 00:50:43.020
comorbidity is a huge issue.

00:50:43.020 --> 00:50:45.960
Anxiety, depression, ASPD, ADHD,

00:50:45.960 --> 00:50:48.677
but all those comorbidities have in common

00:50:48.677 --> 00:50:51.090
impaired social function.

00:50:51.090 --> 00:50:53.010
So I think that's one
thing that runs across

00:50:53.010 --> 00:50:54.630
psychiatric disorders.
- Yeah.

00:50:54.630 --> 00:50:56.607
It could be modeled in animal studies and

00:50:56.607 --> 00:50:58.140
and really help us.

00:50:58.140 --> 00:50:59.640
- Yeah.

00:50:59.640 --> 00:51:01.860
- Yeah, and we frequently,
I will say as the,

00:51:01.860 --> 00:51:04.560
as an animal researcher, we
frequently don't do that.

00:51:04.560 --> 00:51:07.158
And I think that's really, I,

00:51:07.158 --> 00:51:09.960
I couldn't agree with you more
that this is a huge aspect of

00:51:09.960 --> 00:51:12.990
human life of all these disorders
you're talking about and

00:51:12.990 --> 00:51:14.220
also of rat's lives.

00:51:14.220 --> 00:51:15.690
And I don't know if they
have disorders or not,

00:51:15.690 --> 00:51:18.570
but they certainly suffer
from social isolation,

00:51:18.570 --> 00:51:21.360
for example, they care what
other rats think of them,

00:51:21.360 --> 00:51:22.500
that kind of thing.

00:51:22.500 --> 00:51:23.430
And you know,

00:51:23.430 --> 00:51:26.280
how could it be the case that
that's irrelevant for the kind

00:51:26.280 --> 00:51:28.800
of neuroscience studies we're
doing, it seems impossible,

00:51:28.800 --> 00:51:31.230
but I argue that the real reason,

00:51:31.230 --> 00:51:34.020
the main reason why we
don't study these, we,

00:51:34.020 --> 00:51:35.760
I mean I think there's a lot
of neuroscience would agree

00:51:35.760 --> 00:51:37.530
with that statement, but
we still don't study it.

00:51:37.530 --> 00:51:40.290
So why, maybe one of the
reasons is simply that it's,

00:51:40.290 --> 00:51:43.650
it's hard, it's more inconvenient
to add those variables

00:51:43.650 --> 00:51:44.700
into your studies.

00:51:44.700 --> 00:51:48.060
There's not a lot of interest
from funding sources to do

00:51:48.060 --> 00:51:48.893
these, again,

00:51:48.893 --> 00:51:51.090
basic science kinds of questions
to try to understand these

00:51:51.090 --> 00:51:55.260
more difficult but probably
extremely important factors.

00:51:55.260 --> 00:51:57.660
So yeah, I think there's
a growing, as you said,

00:51:57.660 --> 00:52:00.240
a growing group of people that
are starting to realize that

00:52:00.240 --> 00:52:03.210
we are simply missing a huge
part of the puzzle here.

00:52:03.210 --> 00:52:06.630
If we're studying rats in
solitary confinement and seeing

00:52:06.630 --> 00:52:09.000
how much they take drugs, which
they don't know are illegal,

00:52:09.000 --> 00:52:10.380
they're not illegal for them, they're not,

00:52:10.380 --> 00:52:11.760
they don't know anything
about the health consequences.

00:52:11.760 --> 00:52:13.980
Why wouldn't a rat take drugs in my lab?

00:52:13.980 --> 00:52:16.080
You know, there's ways we can get at that.

00:52:16.080 --> 00:52:17.400
We can say, you know,

00:52:17.400 --> 00:52:20.400
you must choose between hanging
out with a buddy and taking

00:52:20.400 --> 00:52:21.390
some drug, for example.
- Yeah.

00:52:21.390 --> 00:52:23.610
- Now we're in the ballpark
of the kind of choices people

00:52:23.610 --> 00:52:25.610
are really making when they're addicted.

00:52:26.855 --> 00:52:27.857
- Yeah, yeah.

00:52:27.857 --> 00:52:29.160
Really well said.

00:52:29.160 --> 00:52:32.460
Well this has been a great
conversation in true form I think

00:52:32.460 --> 00:52:35.460
we've covered a lot of ground
in terms of the work that

00:52:35.460 --> 00:52:38.880
